Morning warehouse organizing:
1. Organized the cable tray and wiring tool area with Zhao Qiang;
2. Demonstrated how to distinguish cable specifications (outer sheath color, printed markings);
3. Used a multimeter's continuity and resistance ranges to verify cables;
4. Xiao Zhao asked about confusing voltage and resistance ranges, so I gave practical measurement demonstrations;
5. Midway, fixed the loose socket on an old power strip.

Afternoon continued:
1. Led Xiao Zhao in inspecting and repairing the distribution box, and removed and replaced the relay with stuck contacts;
2. Sorted tools; rusted parts were uniformly cleaned and oiled or scrapped.

I. This week's study topic: relay protection and PLC control
II. Recorded content:
1. Principle of three-stage current protection in relay protection; drew a mind map;
2. PLC control logic for star-delta step-down starting of the motor; simulated it in software and saved screenshots;
3. Verified it against the logic diagram conceived during the lunch break.
III. Source: 2025-02-13 evening study-room review

Key points recorded with three-color pens:
Red: Characteristics of inrush current — contains a large amount of second harmonic, waveform has a dead angle, initially biased to one side of the time axis;
Blue: Characteristics of fault current — waveform is symmetrical, no dead angle, low harmonic content;
Green: Discrimination methods — using second harmonic restraint, waveform identification, dead-angle principle, etc., to distinguish between inrush current and internal fault current waveforms.

Fault cause: During an internal fault, the heavy-gas relay operated correctly, but the light-gas relay misoperated, possibly due to abnormal oil level or gas accumulation;
Handling procedure: check gas composition, oil level, etc.;
Anti-misoperation measures: regularly vent gas and inspect sealing.
